✅ Advantages
Easy to configure for beginners.
Visual representation of tasks.
No need to learn YAML syntax to configure the pipeline.
Convenient for maintaining existing Classic Release workflows.
❌ Disadvantages
Pipeline configuration is not naturally stored alongside application code.
Changes are harder to review using normal Git pull request workflows.
Reusing configurations across many pipelines can require extra effort.
Manual configuration can become difficult to manage at scale.
📝 What Is a YAML Pipeline?

A YAML Pipeline defines CI/CD workflows in a file, commonly named azure-pipelines.yml, stored in your repository.

Pipeline configuration can be versioned, reviewed, branched, and updated like application code.

🔧 Key Features
📝 Pipeline as Code.
🌿 Git branching and version history.
🔍 Pull request reviews for pipeline changes.
♻️ Reusable templates and shared configuration.
🔄 Automated CI triggers and pull request validation.
🧪 Multi-stage build, test, and deployment workflows.
🔐 Integration with Azure DevOps environments, approvals, and checks.
